  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This handbook is for version of the Gith published prior to Monsters of the Multiverse. For the new versions, see our handbooks for the "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Githyanki",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/races/githyanki/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and the "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Githzerai",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/races/githzerai/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gith are a strange race. They share almost nothing between mechanical their two subraces despite them descending from common ancestry. The one defining trait which they do share is an Intelligence increase. This naturally predisposes both subraces to spellcasting class options, but a single Intelligence increase doesn’t necessary lock you into being a wizard or something."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Gith’s biggest issue is that there is no class where their traits fit\n\tperfectly. They don’t have any truly fantastic class options. They work fine\n\tfor several classes, and they offer some exciting options like a wizard in\n\tmedium armor, but you’ll nearly always find that some major component of your\n\tracial traits are either redundant or ignored."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The custom origin rules allow you to reassign the Gith’s ability score increases, addressing the race’s largest mechanical problem. The Githyanki becomes an appealing choice for poorly-armored spellcasters, and the Githzerai becomes a more niche option with a mix of utility and defensive options. Both subraces’ innate spellcasting offer some additional utility."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
